        <aside class="key-points" aria-labelledby="key-points-title">
          <h2 id="key-points-title">Key points</h2>
          <dl>
            <dt>Question</dt>
            <dd>Why do some people fear learning or using French, and how does that fear affect learning?</dd>

            <dt>Finding</dt>
            <dd>The literature points to a situation-specific anxiety shaped by public performance, anticipated judgment, low perceived competence, perfectionism, classroom climate, and cognitive overload. It appears most visible when learners must understand or speak in real time.</dd>

            <dt>Meaning</dt>
            <dd>Fear is neither proof that French is inherently intimidating nor proof of low aptitude. Anxiety can narrow attention, suppress participation, and create an avoidance cycle; supportive, autonomy-respecting, progressively challenging instruction may interrupt that cycle.</dd>
          </dl>
        </aside>

        <section class="section" id="abstract">
          <p class="section-kicker">01 · Abstract</p>
          <h2>A social-performance problem with cognitive consequences</h2>
          <p><strong>Importance.</strong> Learning French requires learners to perform a still-developing identity in front of other people. Errors are audible, response time is short, and progress is uneven. For some learners, those conditions produce a distinct form of anxiety associated with language learning and use.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1">1</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-2" aria-label="Reference 2">2</a></p>
          <p><strong>Objective.</strong> To synthesize reliable evidence on why people become afraid of learning or using French, how anxiety affects cognition and behavior, and which design principles are most defensible for educators and learning products.</p>
          <p><strong>Evidence review.</strong> This focused narrative review prioritizes peer-reviewed meta-analyses, major conceptual reviews, and primary studies involving learners of French. Publisher databases, scholarly indexes, and reference lists were searched using combinations of <em>French language anxiety</em>, <em>foreign language classroom anxiety</em>, <em>French self-efficacy</em>, <em>willingness to communicate</em>, <em>fear of negative evaluation</em>, and <em>cognitive processing</em>. It is not a registered systematic review and does not estimate prevalence.</p>
          <p><strong>Findings.</strong> Across 97 reports and 19,933 learners of multiple languages, second-language anxiety had a moderate negative association with achievement (<em>r</em> = −.36).<a class="cite" href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3">3</a> French-specific studies add causal and contextual detail: experimentally introducing a video camera increased state anxiety and coincided with vocabulary-learning deficits among 72 first-year university French students;<a class="cite" href="#ref-5" aria-label="Reference 5">5</a> self-efficacy and self-regulation predicted important outcomes in college French;<a class="cite" href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7">7</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-8" aria-label="Reference 8">8</a> and willingness to speak varied with perceived competence, anxiety, peers, and task context.<a class="cite" href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9">9</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-10" aria-label="Reference 10">10</a></p>
          <p><strong>Conclusions and relevance.</strong> The best-supported interpretation is not that learners fear French as a language. They fear what French-learning situations may expose: mistakes, incomprehension, loss of control, unfavorable comparison, and a gap between the self they want to present and the limited language they can currently produce. Anxiety can then consume processing capacity and reduce the practice needed for improvement.</p>

          <details class="method-box">
            <summary>Review scope and source-selection notes</summary>
            <div class="method-content">
              <p>Sources were selected for direct relevance, methodological importance, or French-specific samples. The synthesis distinguishes experimental evidence, longitudinal or repeated observation, cross-sectional association, meta-analysis, and conceptual review.</p>
              <p>Because French-only research is comparatively small and concentrated in university or Canadian school settings, broader second-language anxiety research is used to explain general mechanisms. Those inferences are labeled and should not be mistaken for evidence that French produces more anxiety than another target language.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

