          <figure class="figure" aria-labelledby="figure-three-title">
            <p class="figure-number">Figure 3</p>
            <h3 class="figure-title" id="figure-three-title">A research-informed pathway from social threat to avoidance</h3>
            <div class="pathway">
              <div class="pathway-step">
                <strong>Trigger</strong>
                <span>Cold call, oral test, fast speech, fluent interlocutor, correction, unfamiliar group</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pathway-step">
                <strong>Appraisal</strong>
                <span>“I will fail,” “They will judge me,” or “I cannot respond quickly enough”</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pathway-step">
                <strong>Interference</strong>
                <span>Worry, self-monitoring, narrowed attention, impaired retrieval, physical arousal</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pathway-step">
                <strong>Behavior</strong>
                <span>Silence, short answers, over-rehearsal, avoidance, or withdrawal from future practice</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="feedback">Less successful practice can weaken perceived competence, increasing threat the next time.</div>
            <figcaption>Conceptual synthesis based on Horwitz et al., MacIntyre and Gardner, de Saint Léger and Storch, and MacIntyre and Doucette.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1">1</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-5" aria-label="Reference 5">5</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-6" aria-label="Reference 6">6</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9">9</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-10" aria-label="Reference 10">10</a> The arrows express a plausible process model, not effect sizes from a single study.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>Communication avoidance is especially important because practice is part of the learning mechanism. In Canadian high-school learners of French, anxiety about speaking French was negatively related to willingness to communicate and perceived competence.<a class="cite" href="#ref-10" aria-label="Reference 10">10</a> A learner can therefore value French and intend to speak it while still hesitating at the moment an opportunity appears.</p>
          <p>Recent work also cautions against treating anxiety as a constant. Its intensity can change within seconds as the topic, interlocutor, feedback, or learner appraisal changes.<a class="cite" href="#ref-14" aria-label="Reference 14">14</a> This helps explain why broad labels such as “an anxious learner” can obscure the specific conditions that educators can modify.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="evidence">
          <p class="section-kicker">05 · Evidence map</p>
          <h2>What French-specific studies contribute</h2>
          <p class="lead">The French evidence is meaningful but heterogeneous. It includes one particularly informative experiment and several correlational or repeated-measure studies in college, immersion, and Canadian school contexts.</p>

