        <section class="section" id="experience">
          <p class="section-kicker">06 · Lived pattern</p>
          <h2>How anxiety can appear across the learning cycle</h2>
          <p class="lead">Language anxiety may be visible, but it is often misread as weak motivation, poor preparation, or low aptitude.</p>

          <div class="stage-grid">
            <div class="stage">
              <h3>Before participation</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Excessive rehearsal for a short response</li>
                <li>Dread before class, tutoring, or conversation</li>
                <li>Avoiding tasks that could reveal pronunciation or comprehension gaps</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="stage">
              <h3>During speaking or listening</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Mind going blank despite prior knowledge</li>
                <li>Very short answers, delayed entry, or unusually quiet speech</li>
                <li>Attention shifting from meaning to self-monitoring and error detection</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="stage">
              <h3>After performance</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Rumination about a small error</li>
                <li>Harsh comparison with fluent classmates or native speakers</li>
                <li>Discounting successful communication because the form was imperfect</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="stage">
              <h3>Over time</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Fewer voluntary speaking opportunities</li>
                <li>A gap between receptive knowledge and spontaneous use</li>
                <li>Reduced course persistence or avoidance of higher-stakes contexts</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p style="margin-top: 22px; font-size: 0.82rem;">These patterns synthesize constructs and behaviors reported in foundational, willingness-to-communicate, perfectionism, and dynamic-anxiety research.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1">1</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9">9</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-10" aria-label="Reference 10">10</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-12" aria-label="Reference 12">12</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-14" aria-label="Reference 14">14</a> They are indicators for inquiry, not a checklist for diagnosis.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="implications">
          <p class="section-kicker">07 · Implications</p>
          <h2>Design for credible success, not zero discomfort</h2>
          <p class="lead">The literature supports reducing unnecessary social threat while preserving meaningful challenge. A goal of “no anxiety” is less useful than helping learners act effectively despite manageable uncertainty.</p>

          <div class="implications">
            <h3>Evidence-informed principles for French instruction and learning products</h3>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Stage exposure.</strong> Move from private planning to pair rehearsal, small-group exchange, and only then public performance. The sequence lowers initial evaluation pressure without removing communication.</li>
              <li><strong>Separate fluency from accuracy.</strong> Make it explicit whether a task rewards meaning, accuracy, or both. During fluency work, collect errors for delayed feedback rather than interrupting every utterance.</li>
              <li><strong>Build task-specific efficacy.</strong> Use short, level-matched tasks, progress records, repeated attempts, and concrete “can do” criteria. French research suggests that perceived capability and self-regulation matter alongside anxiety.<a class="cite" href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7">7</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-8" aria-label="Reference 8">8</a></li>
              <li><strong>Return control to the learner.</strong> Offer limited choices of topic, partner, preparation time, or response mode. Give feedback that explains progress and the next attainable step; controlling or uninformative communication can undermine motivation.<a class="cite" href="#ref-11" aria-label="Reference 11">11</a></li>
              <li><strong>Normalize imperfect public language.</strong> Model repair phrases, partial answers, clarification requests, and teacher self-correction. This directly challenges the perfectionist belief that competent speakers do not make visible errors.<a class="cite" href="#ref-12" aria-label="Reference 12">12</a></li>
              <li><strong>Design the peer climate.</strong> Stable partners, cooperative goals, and noncompetitive turn-taking can make participation safer. Peer affiliation and supportive groups influence willingness and enjoyment.<a class="cite" href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9">9</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-13" aria-label="Reference 13">13</a></li>
              <li><strong>Diagnose the moment, not the person.</strong> Ask which skill, task, audience, feedback practice, or timing triggers anxiety. Recent research shows substantial within-person fluctuation.<a class="cite" href="#ref-14" aria-label="Reference 14">14</a></li>
            </ol>
            <p class="practice-note">These are evidence-informed design implications, not a single validated treatment package. Direct intervention trials in French-specific settings remain limited.</p>
          </div>

          <p style="margin-top: 28px;">Positive emotion should not be treated as the simple absence of anxiety. In a large international sample, foreign-language enjoyment and anxiety were related to different learner and classroom factors; learners could experience both. Teacher skill and a supportive peer group were prominent in accounts of enjoyable episodes.<a class="cite" href="#ref-13" aria-label="Reference 13">13</a> Good design can therefore cultivate curiosity, affiliation, and visible progress even when some nervousness remains.</p>

