        <section class="section" id="limitations">
          <p class="section-kicker">08 · Interpretation</p>
          <h2>What the literature cannot yet answer</h2>
          <div class="limitations">
            <h3>Limits of this review and evidence base</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>No French-only prevalence estimate.</strong> The literature does not support a credible statement such as “X% of people are afraid to learn French.”</li>
              <li><strong>Direction of cause is often uncertain.</strong> Anxiety may hinder performance; struggling performance may also create anxiety. Cross-sectional correlations cannot separate the two. The French camera experiment offers stronger causal evidence for an acute effect.<a class="cite" href="#ref-5" aria-label="Reference 5">5</a></li>
              <li><strong>Measurement remains contested.</strong> The FLCAS is widely used, but reviews question whether it cleanly distinguishes anxiety from confidence, attitude, and perceived competence.<a class="cite" href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4">4</a></li>
              <li><strong>French samples are narrow.</strong> Much of the direct evidence comes from North American college students, Canadian adolescents, or immersion courses. Adult beginners, migrants, online learners, and people who never enroll are less visible.</li>
              <li><strong>French is rarely compared head-to-head with other languages.</strong> The review supports mechanisms operating in French contexts, not a claim that French causes more anxiety than Spanish, German, Mandarin, or another language.</li>
              <li><strong>This is a focused narrative review.</strong> It prioritizes influential and directly relevant peer-reviewed studies but is not a preregistered systematic review with duplicate screening and risk-of-bias scoring.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="takeaway" aria-labelledby="takeaway-title">
          <p class="section-kicker">Conclusion</p>
          <h2 id="takeaway-title">The fear is often about exposure, not French</h2>
          <p>People become afraid when using French makes uncertainty public and consequential. Anticipated judgment, low perceived competence, perfectionism, limited control, and real-time processing demands can turn an ordinary learning task into a social threat. Once anxiety disrupts attention or suppresses participation, it can reduce the successful practice that would otherwise build confidence. The practical response is not to remove all difficulty; it is to engineer repeated, socially safe, credible experiences of progress.</p>
        </section>

